/**
 * String Length Recursively
 * Topic: Recursion (Linear on string)
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - Cannot use s.length() or any loop
 * - Empty string → return 0
 *
 * =====================================================================
 * APPROACH — Tail Recursive with Substring
 * =====================================================================
 * WHY: Without length() or loops, only way to reduce string is substring(1)
 *      which removes first character each call.
 *      Accumulator carries running length forward.
 *
 * HOW: Base case → s.isEmpty() → return accLen (holds final length)
 *      Recursive case → remove first char, increment accLen
 *
 * KEY INSIGHT:
 * - Index approach seems cleaner but requires s.length() for base case
 *   which violates the constraint — so substring is the only option.
 * - s.equals("") and s.isEmpty() do exactly the same thing.
 *   Using both with && is redundant — s.isEmpty() alone is sufficient.
 *
 * COMMON MISTAKE 1: substring() → wrong casing, Java uses substring() lowercase s.
 * COMMON MISTAKE 2: Thinking SC is O(n) — substring(1) creates a NEW String
 *   object every call. Total chars across all strings = n+(n-1)+...+1 = n(n+1)/2
 *   → SC = O(n²) not O(n)!
 * COMMON MISTAKE 3: Using && instead of just s.isEmpty() — redundant condition.
 *
 * Call as: length("hello", 0)
 *
 * TC: O(n) — n recursive calls, one per character
 * SC: O(n²) — new String object created each call via substring(1)
 */
public int length(String s, int accLen) {
    if (s.isEmpty()) return accLen;
    return length(s.substring(1), accLen + 1);
}
